  "full_text": "Hi, it's June 17th, and I just wanted to update what I changed on TempleOS. You know how we have a graphics file format called GR? Well, I put a bunch of them together into a... Well, I added a timestamp and an XY into the... graphics file and then what I did is I made so that if you if you just put a bunch of them together into a file then what it does is with the timestamp and the XY and a rectangle then basically you can it's a compressed format that that updates what's changed from frame to frame and so anyway so I made a new so Simply by combining together the gr files. I made a movie file and and then you know, there's the there's the TOA in the in the Linux directory There's a there's a utility called TOSC and you have to compile it in Linux and then once you have that then you can start moving the You can convert the the movie file into bitmap files and then a lot of software accepts a uh a sequence of bitmap files um a numbered sequence so uh um so the the tosc in in the linux directory now does um converts movies to bitmaps bitmap to a sequence of bitmap files and the uh um also another thing i did is i got rid of bmp support um by default You can find it on the supplemental disc. So I removed BMP support. So all we have is the native GR. So that's pretty cool. So anyway, it's cleaning up really nicely. So now I can make screencasts really easily. I think I have the option of putting sound into the movie files. I guess I'm going to do that. It'll be super compressed because all it is is the... frequency as you probably know so it's gonna be crazy how compressed it is anyway so uh I guess I have to make a container or something well anyway so",
